  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "2: Configure tenant settings",
  "nextTour": "3: Test your MCP Server with MCP Inspector",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Promote the connection to a domain-level connection",
      "description": "## Promote the connection to a domain-level connection\n\nTo allow third-party clients like MCP Inspector to use an Auth0 connection such as a username-and-password database or a social connection, you need to promote the connection to a domain-level connection. In this lab, we'll do this for the default Username-Password-Authentication connection.\n\n1. From the Auth0 Dashboard, go to **Authentication** > **Database**.\n2. Select **Username-Password-Authentication**.\n>(Note: the **Username-Password-Authentication** database connection is included on all Auth0 public cloud tenants by default. If you have deleted it, you will need to re-create it.)\n3. Scroll down and toggle on **Promote Connection to Domain Level**."
    },
    {
      "file": "fastmcp-mcp-js-sample/src/auth0.ts",
      "description": "## Create an API integration to represent the MCP server\nSince you have authorized the Auth0 Labs Extension to configure settings on your tenant, we'll use the extension to create an API integration for the sample MCP server.\n\n1. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.tenantConfigure) to run the command that creates the API integration.\n> Wait until you see the message that `resources.xml` has been successfully deployed before continuing to the next step.\n2. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.localConfigure) to set your local environment configurations (creates `.env` file in the `fastmcp-mcp-js-sample` directory).\n\n### What did I just do?\nWe used a bit of automation, so let's step back and review what was done:\n\n1. The Auth0 Labs Extension used details in [resources.yml](./.auth0/lab/resources.yml) with Auth0 CLI to create an API integration that has RBAC enabled and the Permissions claim will be added to any access tokens minted for this API by your Auth0 tenant's Authorization Server. The available permissions for this API are:\n\n    - `tool:whoami` (Grants permission to the WhoAmI tool)\n    - `tool:greet` (Grants permission to the Greeting tool)\n\n2. The Auth0 Labs Extension used the configs specified in [environment.json](./.auth0/lab/environment.json) to create the [.env](./fastmcp-mcp-js-sample/.env) for the MCP server. Notice that the file `fastmcp-mcp-js-sample/src/auth0.ts` has been opened for you here. It uses the environment variables from the `.env` to instantiate a new `ApiClient` using the Auth0 Authentication SDK for APIs on JavaScript runtimes, [@auth0/auth0-api-js](https://github.com/auth0/auth0-auth-js).\n\nNext, we'll create roles to assign these permissions to and a test user to assign roles to.",
      "line": 18,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 15,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 18,
          "character": 43
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Create a test user",
      "description": "## Create a test user\n\nIf you don't have any users in the Username-Password-Authentication connection you can log into, you will need to create a test user. You can skip this step if you already have a user in this connection you can log into.\n\n1. Go to **User Management** > **Users**.\n2. Select **Create User**.\n3. Ensure **User-Password-Authentication** is the selected connection.\n4. Enter an email and password that you can remember for authentication later in this lab."
    },
    {
      "title": "Create roles",
      "description": "## Create roles and assign permissions\n\nThe sample MCP server application in this lab is prepared to show different tools depending on the role of a given user. For example, a **Tool Administrator** will have access to different tools than a **Tool User**.\n\nLet’s create roles that allow you to control which users can access which tools.\n\n\n1. From the Auth0 Dashboard, go to **User Management** > **Roles**.\n2. Select **Create Role**.\n3. Create a new role named **Tool Administrator** with the description **Grants access to all MCP tools**.\n4. Select the **Permissions** tab and add a permission.\n5. Select **MCP Tools API** and select and add all the scopes.\n7. Add another role named **Tool User** with the description **Grants access to basic MCP tools**.\n8. Go to the **Permission tab** and add a permission.\n9. Select **MCP Tools API** and ONLY select and add **tool:whoami**.\n10. Select the **Users** tab, enter the username of the test user you previously created in the Username-Password-Authentication database connection.\n11. Select **Assign**."
    },
    {
      "title": "Recap",
      "description": "## Recap\nYou have successfully created an Auth0 integration for your MCP server with role-based access. You've also pulled down the local configurations needed to run the MCP server. In the next section, you will test your MCP server.",
